    "narrative": "Our top offensive engine for 2026-07-10 is **Josh Naylor** (68.45), who benefits from a prime matchup against Martinez. His ability to capitalize on the pitcher's elevated xERA, combined with favorable platoon and order positioning, makes him the foundational piece of our lineup. Closely trailing is **Isaac Paredes** (68.21), who enjoys an excellent setup against Quantrill; he is a must-start given the high SP-skill advantage and his current reliability in the three-hole. For those chasing our primary objective\u2014stolen bases\u2014**Jose Caballero** (59.91) remains a high-floor play. Despite hitting eighth, his aggressive baserunning profile against a pitcher with a notoriously slow delivery makes him a tactical lock, as does **Seiya Suzuki** (64.86), whose strong projected output against Greene anchors our outfield, even with the looming 35% rain risk.\n\nThe decision to keep **Austin Riley** (57.16) on the bench was the most difficult call of the morning. While he boasts a strong projection, his bottom-of-the-order usage and the presence of elite hold specialists currently mitigating stolen base potential pushed him just behind the efficient production of **Tyler Soderstrom** and **James Wood**. **James Wood** (55.03) remains a start based on his elite xwOBA (0.432), even though his SB upside is capped today against Weathers. We are cautiously monitoring **Seiya Suzuki** due to the weather; should that game get washed out, we will rotate **Austin Riley** into the active slot to maintain our offensive floor. Across the rest of the board, we expect **Kyle Karros** (63.20) to leverage the wind conditions in his favor, while **Travis Bazzana** (57.03) offers a solid lead-off advantage that should help accumulate Runs regardless of the park's slight negative adjustment.",
